(Description)
- Given a collection of candidate numbers (candidates) and a target number (target), find all unique combinations in candidates where the candidate numbers sum to target.
- Each number in candidates may only be used once in the combination.
- Note: The solution set must not contain duplicate combinations.

--- Solution ---
class Solution {
    List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();

    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum2(int[] candidates, int target) {
        Arrays.sort(candidates);
        findCombination(candidates, new ArrayList<>(), 0, target);
        return result;
    }

    private void findCombination(int[] candidates, List<Integer> temp, int position, int target) {        
        if (target == 0) {
            result.add(new ArrayList<>(temp));
            return;
        }
        if (position >= candidates.length) {
            return;
        }
        for (int i = position; i < candidates.length; i++) {
            if (i > position && candidates[i] == candidates[i-1]) {
                continue;
            }
            if (candidates[i] > target) {
                break;
            }
            temp.add(candidates[i]);
            findCombination(candidates, temp, i + 1, target - candidates[i]);
            temp.remove(temp.size()-1);
        }
    }
}

--- Approach ---
(Recursion)
From first int to the last, recursion subtract current value from the given target,
so that if the target becomes 0, it add the current combination into the result.
- One rule was that this combination don't require the duplicate numbers, therefore the algorithm skips the recursion if current number is same with the previous one.
- Another thing that I should consider in debugging process was ArrayList all of the recursions share the ArrayList. => ArrayList had to be copied when it was added to the result ArrayList.
